<onlyinclude>'''Digital signal processors''' specialize the execution and memory organization of programmable computing for repetitive, often latency-sensitive operations on sampled data. Their distinguishing mechanisms are architectural rather than a unique mathematical instruction set: multiply–accumulate datapaths, address-generation facilities, local memories, and explicit instruction-level parallelism can reduce instruction overhead and data-movement costs for suitable kernels.</onlyinclude>

'''Reading depth:''' [[Digital signal processor/Simple|Simple]] · [[Digital signal processor/Technical|Technical]]

== Computational workload ==
For an <math>L</math>-tap finite impulse response (FIR) filter, an output sample can be written
:<math>y[n]=\sum_{k=0}^{L-1}h[k]x[n-k].</math>
The direct form requires <math>L</math> products and <math>L-1</math> additions per output, assuming an initially zero accumulator or equivalent reduction. A MAC instruction or fused arithmetic datapath can perform the product and accumulation as a closely coupled operation. This is an operation count, not a guarantee of one output per <math>L</math> cycles: instruction issue, vector lanes, pipeline overlap, loads, and memory stalls determine realized throughput.

A circular buffer retains a finite history of samples; modulo addressing can avoid explicit branch or pointer-reset instructions. This is implementation-dependent. For example, Texas Instruments documents circular addressing on a subset of address registers of its C67x architecture, rather than every register.<ref name="ti-isa">Texas Instruments, ''TMS320C67x/C67x+ DSP CPU and Instruction Set Reference Guide'', SPRU733A, §§2.7.3 and 3.8, https://www.ti.com/lit/ug/spru733a/spru733a.pdf</ref>

== Architecture and memory ==
Common DSP implementations use separate or concurrently accessible instruction and data pathways, multiple arithmetic units, DMA engines, and explicitly managed local storage. The TI C67x family provides a concrete case with two multipliers and six other arithmetic/logic functional units, but that organization is not a universal DSP definition.<ref name="ti-isa"/> Wide-issue or VLIW implementations expose independent operations to a compiler; dependence chains and memory conflicts limit utilization. Software-pipelined loops can overlap successive iterations, subject to initiation interval and resource constraints.<ref name="ti-opt">Texas Instruments, ''Introduction to TMS320C6000 DSP Optimization'', SPRABF2 (2011), https://www.ti.com/lit/an/sprabf2/sprabf2.pdf</ref>

== Numerical representation ==
Fixed-point designs represent values with an implied scale, often enabling efficient integer datapaths but requiring explicit attention to dynamic range, saturation, rounding, and overflow. Floating-point hardware offers a larger representable dynamic range, generally at implementation-dependent cost in area, power, or latency. Neither arithmetic format guarantees numerical accuracy: quantization error, filter stability, and accumulated rounding error depend on algorithm and implementation.

== Illustrative calculation ==
For a hypothetical 32-tap direct FIR filter at 48,000 output samples per second, the nominal arithmetic demand is <math>32\times48{,}000=1{,}536{,}000</math> multiplications and <math>31\times48{,}000=1{,}488{,}000</math> additions per second, before any multichannel processing, data transfer, or control overhead. The figure is an algorithmic workload estimate, not a benchmark of a specific chip.

== FIR implementation and numerical constraints ==
For a streaming <math>L</math>-tap FIR with one new input every <math>T_s</math> seconds, the sustained compute schedule must produce an output within the applicable sample or block deadline. A simple non-overlapped implementation with <math>f_{\mathrm{clk}}</math> clock cycles per second and <math>C</math> cycles per output requires <math>C/f_{\mathrm{clk}}\leq T_s</math>; this inequality must be adjusted for block buffering, pipeline latency and concurrent tasks. It is a necessary deadline test for that execution model, not a general proof of schedulability.

The direct FIR count assumes an initially zero accumulator: <math>L</math> multiplications and <math>L-1</math> additions. An implementation that initializes an accumulator with a bias or zero and issues <math>L</math> MACs can have a different instruction count without changing the mathematical sum. Fixed-point accumulators require sufficient headroom: for signed integer samples and coefficients, the worst-case absolute sum is bounded by <math>\sum_k |h[k]|\,|x[n-k]|</math>, before scaling and rounding. Saturation, intermediate precision and coefficient quantization must be specified to reproduce the numerical result.

On the documented C67x family, circular addressing is available only for A4–A7 and B4–B7 via AMR, and buffer sizes are specified in bytes, not element counts; alignment and size constraints matter. For example, an eight-element buffer of 32-bit samples occupies 32 bytes. This is a concrete ISA constraint, not a property of all DSPs.<ref name="ti-isa"/>

== Latency, throughput, and energy ==
Real-time viability depends on worst-case processing latency relative to the sample or block deadline, not only average operations per second. DMA contention, cache misses, interrupts, and memory layout can produce latency spikes. Power comparisons must hold workload, numerical accuracy, technology, clocking, and software maturity constant. CPUs, GPUs, microcontrollers, and neural accelerators may overlap DSP workloads; the best implementation depends on the system constraints.

== Measurement and research questions ==
A meaningful benchmark identifies the exact DSP model and clock, compiler flags, memory placement, input rate, filter length, numerical format and tolerated error. Measure steady-state cycles per output, worst-case deadline misses, DMA contention, and energy over a stated workload; separate cold-start and warm-cache cases. Relevant research problems include memory-aware scheduling for heterogeneous DSP/NPU pipelines, reproducible worst-case latency under shared-memory contention, and mixed-precision filters with bounded error. These are proposed evaluation directions, not experimentally established improvements.

== Provenance and limitations ==
The architectural examples above are grounded in Texas Instruments C67x documentation and the C6000 optimization application report; they demonstrate one processor family, not the capabilities of all DSPs. The FIR arithmetic is a directly derived illustrative model. No universal energy-efficiency or speed advantage is inferred from these documents.

